./docs/novelty-sweep.md:29:| 8 | **arXiv:1406.6051** — Kim, Tserkovnyak & Tchernyshyov, *Propulsion of a domain wall in an antiferromagnet by magnons*, PRB 90, 104406 (2014) ✅ verified | close | **downgrade → adjacent** | The sweep summarized this as magnons producing "net, persistent motion/displacement" of the wall. The verified abstract says something meaningfully different: magnons pass a *stationary* wall **without reflection and exert no force on it**; propulsion arises only via a "redshift" mechanism once the wall precesses, under a sustained magnon current. That is steady-state driving, not a one-shot permanent displacement from a single wavepacket — a different observable from the campaign's memory effect. Real threat is lower than stated; #2 is the paper to worry about. |
